[;Taylor_series
 Let {f(x)} have derivatives of all order at {x=c},
 The Taylor series of {f(x)} centered at {c} is
 { f(x) = sum__(i=0)^infty frac(1,i!) f^((i))(c) (x-c)^i }
 
 [todo The taylor series of a any polynomial is the polynomial itself
  [For example, for the polynomial { f(x) = x^3 + 2 x^2 + 3 x + 1 }, let's say {c=0}
   { f^((0))(0) = 1 }, which is the constant term
   { f^((1))(0) = (x => 3 x^2 + 4 x + 3)(0) = 3 }, which is the linear coefficient
   { f^((2))(0) = (x => 6 x + 4)(0) = 4 }, which is the quadratic coefficient, multiplied by 2
   { f^((3))(0) = (x => 6)(0) = 4 }, which is the cubic coefficient, multiplied by 6
   
   so you can see how the {frac(1,i!)} plays into this whole story]
  
  [todo What about for arbitrary center {c}?
   ]
  ]
 ]

[;chain_rule
 {
  diff(f circ g)(x)
  = diff(f)(g(x)) diff(g)(x)
 }
 another way to say it
 {
  frac(dz,dx) = frac(dz,dy) frac(dy,dx)
 }
 ]
